<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Enki</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Enki was the Sumerian god of fresh water, crafts, wisdom and magic, known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadians</a> as Ea. His chief temple, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/e-abzu">E-abzu</a>, stood at Eridu in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, which was regarded as his city. In myths he appears as a creator and helper of humanity, and in Enki and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninhursag">Ninhursag</a> he provides the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilmun">Dilmun</a> with fresh water.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enki.jpg" alt="Enki" width="265" height="306" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enki(Ea).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Enki was first worshipped in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>. Early sources of the Fāra period associate him with Eridu, one of the oldest cities of Sumer, which was regarded as his home from the Early Dynastic Period. His temple there was the E-Abzû, the House of the Abzû, and he was called king of the Abzû and king of Eridu. He was already a major deity at the time of the earliest written sources.</p><p>Ea was originally a separate god, whose name is of unknown, possibly Semitic origin and first appears in personal names of the Old Akkadian period. The two were already syncretized in parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> in the second half of the third millennium BCE, though it is uncertain when they were fully equated. The name Enki is usually translated as Lord of the Earth, but not all scholars accept this.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Enki was worshipped as the god of wisdom, crafts, fresh subterranean waters, springs and, by extension, incantations and magic, and as a creator. He was believed to reside in the Abzû, imagined as a region of fresh water beneath the earth. His symbols included the goat-fish, the ram-headed staff and the turtle. He is one of the few Mesopotamian deities with a recognisable iconography: streams of water flow from his body, often with fish in them, a feature first attested in the Old Akkadian Period.</p><p>His wife was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damkina">Damgalnuna</a>, and their children included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a>, the patron god of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, and Asalluhi, a god of incantations later merged with Marduk. His attendant was Isimud, who is shown with two faces. The sky god An is named as his father in several sources.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>His cult was particularly influential in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Ur III</a> and Old Babylonian periods, when he formed a triad at the top of the pantheon with Anu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a>, and was attested in almost all important cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>. It spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> early in the second millennium BCE, and into Hurrian and Hittite religion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, where he could be invoked as a divine witness to treaties. With the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> his cult lost importance, but he remained a major god of first millennium BCE Babylonia.</p><p>In myth he is a crafty counsellor who solves difficult problems. Tradition regarded him as the creator of humankind from clay. In Atra-hasis he works with the mother goddess to create people and later opposes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a>'s attempts to destroy them in the flood. Berossos identified Ea with Kronos.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Enki” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Religion centred on the god Inzak, with his consort Meskilak, and on <b>Enki</b>, the Sumerian god of fresh water; the temple at Barbar was built around a sacred spring-fed pool.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Mesopotamian religion was polytheistic and also henotheistic: the people of a city held its tutelary god above the others, as <b>Enki</b> at Eridu, Enlil at Nippur, Ishtar at Arbela, Marduk at Babylon and Ashur at Assur.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesopotamian-religion">Mesopotamian religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Utnapishtim tells how the god <b>Enki</b> warned him to build a boat, how after the storm all human beings had turned to clay, and how he released a dove, a swallow and a raven.</blockquote><a href="/work/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>The words printed here are its prologue of 26 lines, on the beginnings of the world and <b>Enki</b>'s voyage by boat to the netherworld; the story then turns to the huluppu tree that Gilgamesh fells for Inanna.</blockquote><a href="/songs/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a></li><li><blockquote>The hymn praises the goddess and gives her a family history found nowhere else: although her parents were <b>Enki</b> and Ninti, the hymn says she was raised by Ninhursag, a goddess not otherwise linked with raising children.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ninkasi">Hymn to Ninkasi</a></li><li><blockquote>The city was above all a sacred place, the seat of <b>Enki</b> (Akkadian Ea), god of fresh water, crafts and wisdom, whose temple was called the E-abzu.</blockquote><a href="/state/eridu">Eridu</a></li></ul></section>
